r {number}, the value of the digit {digit} in the {place} place is {fill_value}\", \"{question_text} The answer is {fill_value}.\", \"{statement}. True or False?\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"F13 and F14 draw place names from PLACE_NAMES, which includes hundreds and thousands. Those are outside this 2-digit lc, and hundreds is in NOT_YET_KNOWN. F13 appears in correct_answer and mcq_options with no bound on the number, so a keyed answer of hundreds is printable. F14 prints the same words in hints, and F03 and F06 print them in hints as relevant_places, so the hint text is affected too. F07 teaches multiplication (multiplication is in NOT_YET_KNOWN, and the template uses the multiply word and the times sign) and draws its v from PLACE_VALUES, which includes 100 and 1000. F17 fill_value draws distractor members including 5 and 4 with no tie to the digit, and number 45, digit 5, place ones, fill 5 prints a true statement that is listed as a distractor. Its true-keyed member 0 is not tied to digit 0, so a false statement can be keyed true. F18 nests F20 or F22 as a question and then prints The answer is with a fill that includes 5 and other values that can be the true answer, so a true statement can be keyed as a distractor. F19 prints a question as a True or False statement, and because F18 ends in a period, the output has a double period before True or False. The other families (F01, F02, F04, F05, F08 to F12, F15, F16, F20 to F22) print text inside the gates and their slot domains do not show a wrong or out-of-scope value, so they are not listed as failing.\"}",
